// Package gen is what a target package says about what it generates.
//
// A language or platform package declares a node of the build-target tree —
// `component go(…) build.language`, `component html(…) build.platform` — and
// the marks here are written on that declaration: which SNGL constructs the
// target emits natively, and which lowering passes it asks for. They used to
// be a Go method compiled into the plugin, which a target that is a command
// rather than a linked-in package cannot answer.
//
// The audience is the plugin set, in this repository or outside it, which is
// why the tier is not under `internal/`. It is not `sngl:build`, whose
// audience is the same: that package is the build-target *tree*, and what
// generating for a target involves is a different subject with more than one
// member in it.
//
// **A capability not written is not held.** There is no base set to subtract
// from: a target that says nothing gets every lowering pass, which is the
// answer that leaves a program correct rather than one that claims a host can
// emit something it cannot. It is also what a plugin answering nothing at all
// gets, which is the case the whole tier exists for.
//
// **A platform builds its widgets in SNGL.** Beside each `#[intrinsic]`
// component it declares, a platform writes the functions that make the
// widget, put a child in and take one out, write a prop and connect an event
// the host raises on its own, each marked `#[gen.protocol(op)]` and calling
// the host through a `#[cnative]` or `#[go.native]` declaration; once, beside
// its build-target node, it writes the `connect` every other handler goes
// through. The build splices each where the program makes the operation, so
// a string handed to C is converted and released by the lowering passes, and
// the interpreter's `--simulate` runs the same calls. What is left to the
// platform's generator is where each widget lives in the code it writes.
